        "en": {
          "why_it_matters": "The mentoring architecture is the reason this belongs, not name recognition by itself. Gilmore’s rhythmic language is placed beside a 21-year-old bassist already working in the Immanuel Wilkins orbit, while Joel Ross adds vibraphone to an unusually open drums-bass-mallet configuration. In Jerry’s Place at the Louis Armstrong Center, the apprenticeship idea also lands inside a room built around jazz memory rather than as an abstract conservatory exercise. The instrumentation leaves time, pulse, harmonic implication and interaction unusually exposed.",
          "trip_verdict": "If a New York day already reaches Queens on September 5, attend if convenient; the free afternoon format makes it easy to fold into the day. I would not make a dedicated DMV-to-New-York trip for this date alone because the same trio has another New York performance five days later and the mentorship continues as a four-concert mini-tour.",
